      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          If you searched for a MaxKare treadmill, you most likely saw one on Walmart.com: a folding
          machine with a 3.5 HP badge, 15 incline levels and a price well under the big brands. The
          brand is real, it does sell those machines, and some of them are in stock. What it does not
          have is any independent record of what its treadmills are actually built from. This guide
          sets out who MaxKare is, what it sells now, which listing figures contradict each other,
          what the warranty really says, and who should buy something else.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is a MaxKare Treadmill Any Good? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A MaxKare treadmill is a budget folding machine sold mainly through Walmart.com and
            MaxKare’s own store; it can suit light walking and occasional jogging, but none of its
            figures has been independently confirmed, several listings contradict themselves, and the
            warranty terms are vague.</strong> Buy one only cheaply, from a seller whose return window
            you have read, and with the warranty in writing.
          </p>
          <p>
            The current range runs from a 20-inch-wide folding treadmill listed with a 3.5 HP motor,
            a 12 mph top speed and 15 levels of automatic incline, down to 17-inch-wide machines with
            9 mph tops and walking pads that double as small treadmills. On paper that is a lot of
            machine for the money. In practice, the motor figures are not stated as continuous
            ratings, one listing gives two different weight limits for the same treadmill, and the
            store’s warranty page names no warranty length at all, even though a banner on the same
            site advertises an 18-month warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of that means the machines are bad. It means the useful way to judge a MaxKare
            treadmill is the way you would judge any marketplace brand: read the listing as a set of
            claims, decide whether the price is fair if the claims turn out optimistic, and make sure
            you can send it back if it is not what you expected. The sections below do that reading
            for you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-sells-maxkare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Is MaxKare, and Where Is It Sold?</h2>
<p>
            MaxKare describes itself on its own website as “an ecommerce retailer” serving customers
            in home comfort, relaxation and massagers, heated products and baby care. Treadmills are a
            small part of a catalogue that also includes electric blankets, heating pads, foot spas,
            massagers, ellipticals and basketball hoops. The site does not name a parent company, a
            factory or a US office address, and we could not confirm who owns the brand. A brand press
            release from October 2025 promoting MaxKare products in a Walmart sales event gave a media
            contact at a different company’s email domain, which suggests a wider group of brands
            behind it, but we could not establish the corporate relationship and it does not change
            anything about the purchase.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where you can buy one</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Walmart.com</strong> is where most MaxKare treadmills are sold and where most
            people meet the brand. The October 2025 press release described MaxKare’s deals as
            available exclusively at Walmart, and MaxKare treadmills appear in Walmart’s incline and
            folding treadmill categories. Walmart.com includes third-party marketplace sellers as
            well as Walmart itself, so check who the seller is on the listing you are looking at,
            because that decides who handles a return.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>MaxKare’s own store</strong> (maxkare.net) lists more than a dozen treadmills and
            walking pads, although on the day we checked only four treadmill listings were marked as
            available. Store prices were higher than the Walmart prices that search results showed
            for the same models, which is common for marketplace brands that treat their own site
            as a list-price reference.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Amazon</strong> is not, as far as we could find, a current channel. We searched
            Amazon’s US product data on 14 October 2026 and found no MaxKare treadmill listed, only
            unrelated products. Older MaxKare models were reviewed several years ago by sites that
            linked to Amazon, so the brand may have sold there in the past. If you do find a listing
            using the name, check the seller and the model number carefully, since a missing brand
            is often filled by look-alike products.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are deciding between the two main retailers in general rather than for this brand,
            our guides to
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a>
            and to
            <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policies</a>
            explain how marketplace sellers change who stands behind the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="current-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Current MaxKare Treadmill Range</h2>
<p>
            These are the treadmills and walking pads in MaxKare’s own store on 14 October 2026, with
            the figures the store listing gives and whether the listing was marked as available.
            Every figure is MaxKare’s claim. None has been confirmed by an independent source, and
            we have not tested any of these machines.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model (as listed)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Listed figures</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Store status</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20-inch folding treadmill, 3.5 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 48 in. running surface, 0.6-12 mph, 15 auto incline levels, 300 lb in the title (286 lb in the text)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Available, $699.99-$799.99 across three listings</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.0 HP folding treadmill, 15 levels</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 in. wide belt, 0.6-10 mph, 15 auto incline levels, 300 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Available, $799.99</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15-level auto incline treadmill, 3.0 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">42.5 x 17 in. belt, 0.6-9 mph, 15 auto incline levels, 286 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Available, $599.99</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12-level auto incline treadmill, 2.5 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">17 in. wide belt, up to 8.5 mph, 12 incline levels, 220 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not available</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2-in-1 walking pad, 3.0 HP, touch screen</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.5-4 mph walking, 0.5-9 mph running, 286 lb in the title (265 lb in the text)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not available</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2-in-1 auto incline walking pad, 3.0 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.5-9 mph, 12 incline levels, 286 lb, folds to 6.2 in. high</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not available</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.5 HP walking pad</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">42.2 x 16.5 in. belt, 0.6-7.5 mph with rail up, 265 lb, 4.5 in. high</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not available</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Ultra-thin walking pad, 2.25 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">38.6 x 15.2 in. running area, 0.6-3.8 mph, 265 lb, 38.6 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not available</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two things stand out. The first is that only the full-size folding treadmills were in
            stock at MaxKare’s own store; every walking pad was marked unavailable. Walmart stock can
            differ, but if you were hoping for a MaxKare walking pad, expect to hunt for one.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second is that the store prices are list prices in practice. The 20-inch model is
            listed three times at three prices, and Walmart listings that appeared in search results
            showed the same model for substantially less. Treat any “was” price on a MaxKare listing
            as a reference point rather than a measure of the discount, and compare against similar
            machines in our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$500 guide</a>
            before deciding the deal is a good one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-the-listings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Where MaxKare Listings Contradict Themselves</h2>
<p>
            Budget treadmill listings are written to sell, and figures drift as listings are copied
            between models and marketplaces. MaxKare’s are a clear example. These are the
            inconsistencies we found in the brand’s own store on the day we checked.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Two weight limits for one treadmill</h3>
          <p>
            The 20-inch, 3.5 HP model is titled as a 300 lb treadmill, and one of its three listings
            repeats 300 lb in its specification block. The same listing’s feature text says the belt
            is “designed for users up to 286lbs”. One of the walking pads does the same thing in the
            other direction: 286 lb in the title, “can hold up to 265LBS” in the text. When a seller
            gives two limits, plan around the lower one, and leave a margin below it as well. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            explains why the rating is a boundary rather than a target.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Incline in levels, degrees and percent</h3>
          <p>
            MaxKare’s listings describe incline in three different ways. The 20-inch model has “15
            Levels” and, in an older listing, a “15%” maximum. The 3.0 HP, 286 lb model has 15 levels
            described as “0-15° electric slope”. One auto-incline walking pad has “0-12°” in one
            paragraph and “1-12%” in another. Degrees and percent are not the same thing: 15 degrees
            is roughly a 27% grade, which no budget folding treadmill offers, so the degree figures
            are almost certainly mislabelled levels or percent. Our explainer on
            <a href="/treadmill-incline-percent-vs-degrees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline percent versus degrees</a>
            sets out the conversion. The practical reading is that these machines offer a modest
            powered incline in numbered steps, and the top step is probably around 12 to 15 percent.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Horsepower without a continuous rating</h3>
          <p>
            The listings give motor figures of 2.25 to 3.5 HP. Only one, a walking pad, says what
            kind of figure it is: “DC motor, peak power 3.0HP”. Peak ratings describe a brief maximum,
            not what the motor can sustain for an hour of walking, and they are always larger than
            the continuous (CHP) figure better-documented brands publish. A MaxKare 3.5 HP motor and a
            3.5 CHP motor on a mid-range treadmill are not comparable numbers. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains the difference.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Marketing figures with no method</h3>
          <p>
            The 20-inch model claims its shock absorption “reduces joint impact by 30%”; a walking pad
            claims 40%. Neither says what was measured or how. Treat them as adjectives. The same goes
            for “commercial-grade motor” on a treadmill sold for a few hundred dollars, and for
            “ultra-quiet”, which no MaxKare listing backs with a decibel figure or a test condition.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="twenty-inch-model" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The 20-Inch, 3.5 HP Model: The One Most Searches Mean</h2>
<p>
            The MaxKare treadmill that turns up most often is the 20-inch folding model: a 3.5 HP
            motor badge, 0.6 to 12 mph, 15 levels of powered incline, a 20 by 48-inch running surface,
            60 preset programs, Bluetooth with Zwift named as compatible, and a soft-drop folding
            deck. MaxKare’s store and the Walmart listings that appear in search results agree on
            those headline figures. That agreement is between two copies of the same seller’s
            listing, so it tells you the seller is consistent, not that the figures have been checked.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Twenty inches wide, but only 48 inches long</h3>
          <p>
            The 20-inch width is genuinely generous at this price, and it is the right figure to care
            about if you drift sideways while walking. The 48-inch length is the catch. Forty-eight
            inches is a walking length. Most adults need 55 inches or more to jog comfortably and 60
            inches to run, and the 12 mph top speed is a long way beyond what a 48-inch belt can
            safely accommodate for most people. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            works through stride length against belt length. The short version is that this is a
            wide walking treadmill with a fast top speed, not a running treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Incline, programs and apps</h3>
          <p>
            Powered incline at this price is a real advantage over manual-incline budget machines,
            because you can change it without stepping off. The 60 programs and the body-fat function
            are the kind of features budget consoles add cheaply; body-fat readings from handlebar
            sensors are rough estimates at best. The listing names Zwift and Bluetooth. Whether a
            given budget treadmill reports speed reliably to Zwift varies, and a listing that names an
            app is not a guarantee of a smooth connection. Our
            <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill guide</a>
            explains how those connections work and what to check.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Size and folding</h3>
          <p>
            MaxKare lists the 20-inch model at about 63.6 inches long, 30.5 inches wide and 49.6
            inches high, folding to about 35 inches long. No listing we read gave the machine’s own
            weight, which matters for moving it and as a rough guide to how much steel is underneath.
            Ask the seller before you buy, and check our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space requirements guide</a>
            for the clearance you need behind and beside any treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. MaxKare Walking Pads and 2-in-1 Machines</h2>
<p>
            MaxKare has sold several walking pads: an ultra-thin under-desk pad listed at 4.7 inches
            high and 38.6 lb with a 3.8 mph ceiling, a 2.5 HP pad with a fold-up rail, and two 2-in-1
            machines with touch-screen consoles that run to 9 mph with the rail raised. On the day we
            checked, every one of them was marked unavailable in MaxKare’s own store.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you find one in stock on Walmart, the same reading applies as for the treadmills. The
            2-in-1 models list a walking mode limited to about 4 mph with the rail down and a running
            mode to 9 mph with it up, a split that is common on this kind of machine and sensible for
            safety. One of them gives two different weight limits. None gives a running-surface length
            for the 2-in-1 models in the text we read, which is the figure that decides whether the
            9 mph mode is usable at all. A 2-in-1 pad short enough to slide under a desk is short for
            jogging; treat the running mode as occasional.
          </p>
          <p>
            For under-desk walking specifically, our
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmill guide</a>
            covers better-documented pads, and the
            <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a>
            explains which pad specifications to trust and which to discount.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-and-returns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. MaxKare Warranty, Returns and Support</h2>
<p>
            This is the part of a MaxKare purchase that most deserves attention before you order.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The warranty length is not stated where it should be</h3>
          <p>
            A banner on MaxKare’s store advertises an “18-Month Warranty”. The store’s warranty page,
            which is the document that would govern a claim, does not give a length. It says MaxKare
            “will replace any product found to be defective due to manufacturer flaws”, that refunds
            are available within the first 30 days, that the warranty covers personal use only and is
            not transferable, and that there are “no warranties other than the warranties expressly
            set forth with each product”. The treadmill listings we read did not set one out. So the
            18-month figure is a marketing line we could not find written into terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before buying, ask the seller you are buying from, in writing, how long the warranty runs
            on the frame, the motor and the electronics, whether it covers parts only or labour too,
            and who pays to ship a faulty part or machine. Keep the reply with your receipt. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            lists the questions in full.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Returns from MaxKare’s own store</h3>
          <p>
            MaxKare’s refund policy offers a refund within 30 days of receiving the product for
            items without “man-made damage”, but the buyer pays the return shipping, which is not
            refunded and on a treadmill is substantial. You must email for a return address first.
            And the policy states that “only regular priced items may be refunded, unfortunately sale
            items cannot be refunded”. On a brand whose products are nearly always on sale, that line
            matters: read it as meaning a discounted treadmill bought from the store may not be
            returnable for a change of mind.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Buying on Walmart.com instead</h3>
          <p>
            If you buy through Walmart.com, the return terms depend on whether the seller is Walmart
            or a marketplace seller, and on the category’s rules on the day you buy. Check the return
            window shown on the listing, whether a large item can be returned to a store or must be
            collected, and who pays for that. The shipping page on MaxKare’s own store says it ships
            from warehouses in the United States and Canada, with delivery taking from 2 to 15 days.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Support and parts</h3>
          <p>
            MaxKare’s contact page directs support requests to email. There is no published parts
            catalogue, which is normal for marketplace brands and means that a failed motor controller
            after the warranty period is likely to end the machine’s life rather than lead to a
            repair. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">repair guide</a>
            explains when a repair is worth it on a budget treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety-record" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Recalls: What the CPSC Record Shows</h2>
<p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission’s recall database for MaxKare on
            14 October 2026. There is no recall of a MaxKare treadmill or walking pad, and no CPSC
            warning about MaxKare fitness equipment.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is one MaxKare recall, of a different product. In June 2025 the CPSC announced a
            recall of about 8,560 MaxKare electric blankets, model numbers HB18A-7284-1, HB18A-8490-2
            and HB18A-6284-1, manufactured by Shenzhen Yumo Commerce and Trade Corporation and sold
            exclusively on Walmart.com from June 2021 to November 2024, because they could overheat
            and ignite. The remedy was a refund. It has no bearing on the treadmills, but it does show
            that MaxKare products are made by contract manufacturers and sold through Walmart, which
            fits everything else on this page.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever brand you buy, the safety basics are the same: use the safety key, keep children
            and pets away from a running or plugged-in machine, unplug before any maintenance, and
            stop using a treadmill that surges in speed, smells of burning or trips a breaker. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety guide</a>
            covers the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="older-models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Older MaxKare Treadmills People Still Search For</h2>
<p>
            Several MaxKare models have come and gone, and some still circulate on resale sites. The
            ones you are most likely to meet are the early manual-incline folding treadmills, which
            independent coverage described several years ago.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The 2.5 HP and 1.5 HP manual-incline folders.</strong> One independent review of
            an early MaxKare folding treadmill described a 2.5 HP machine with a 16 by 46-inch running
            belt, a three-position manual incline that you set by stepping off and adjusting the base,
            15 preset programs, a hydraulic soft-drop fold and a 220 lb limit, with a 1.5 HP sibling
            that topped out at 7.5 mph. A listing still on MaxKare’s store for a similar 2.5 HP
            manual-incline machine gives a 17 by 43-inch belt instead, under a different vendor name,
            so belt sizes varied between versions. If you are looking at one used, measure the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The 12% auto-incline 2.5 HP treadmill.</strong> An older listing for a 2.5 HP
            folding treadmill with 0-12% powered incline, an 8.5 mph top speed and a 17-inch belt
            remains on the store but is marked unavailable. Powered incline is the feature most
            likely to fail on any budget treadmill, because the lift motor and its sensor are cheap
            parts doing heavy work, so run a used example through its full incline range before you
            pay.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two versions of the manual-incline machine.</strong> A second independent
            write-up of a MaxKare manual-incline folder described a 17-inch belt, three manual incline
            levels and a 220 lb limit, which matches the 17-inch listing rather than the 16-inch one
            above. That is a reminder that the same product name has covered different hardware over
            the years.
          </p>
          <p>
            For any used MaxKare, the rules are the same as for any discontinued budget treadmill:
            see it run through its speed and incline range, check the belt and deck for wear, and pay
            a price that makes sense if the controller fails next month. Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            walks through the inspection.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strengths-and-weaknesses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. What MaxKare Does Well, and Less Well</h2>
<p>
            <strong>What it does well.</strong> MaxKare offers features at a low price that more
            established brands charge more for: a 20-inch belt width, powered incline, a folding deck
            with a soft-drop mechanism, Bluetooth and a remote on the walking pads. The machines are
            sold largely pre-assembled. And there is no subscription: nothing on a MaxKare listing
            requires a paid app to use the treadmill, which our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            treats as a real advantage for buyers who want to keep running costs at zero.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it does less well.</strong> Documentation. The motor figures are not
            continuous ratings, the weight limits contradict each other, the incline is described in
            three incompatible units, the machine weight of the flagship model is not given, and the
            warranty length is advertised on a banner but missing from the terms. There is no
            independent review of any current model that we could find, no parts catalogue, and
            support is by email. Returns from the brand’s own store put the shipping cost on you and
            exclude sale items.
          </p>
          <p>
            Put together, that makes MaxKare a reasonable gamble at the right price and a poor one at
            the wrong price. At the bottom of its Walmart price range, a MaxKare folding treadmill
            costs little more than a basic walking pad and offers more. At its own store’s list
            prices, it competes with machines from brands that publish continuous motor ratings and
            longer, written warranties.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Who Should Buy a MaxKare Treadmill, and What to Read Next</h2>
<p>
            <strong>It suits a walker on a tight budget who wants incline.</strong> If your plan is
            brisk walking and incline walking a few times a week, a MaxKare folding treadmill at a low
            Walmart price can do that, and the wide belt helps. Our
            <a href="/what-incline-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to walking incline</a>
            covers how to use it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Runners should buy something else.</strong> A 48-inch belt is too short for
            running whatever the top speed says. Look for 55 to 60 inches of belt in our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running treadmill guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Heavier users should leave a wide margin.</strong> With a 286 lb figure sitting
            beside the 300 lb one, and a motor rated only by its peak, a user anywhere near either
            number is asking a lot of the machine. Start with our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for heavier users</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Anyone who wants to buy on Amazon, or wants written cover, should compare
            first.</strong> Our pages on the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills on Amazon</a>
            and the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-300/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $300</a>
            list machines whose figures we could cross-check, and the
            <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon T101 review</a>
            covers an entry-level folding treadmill from a brand with published warranty terms.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


