        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes MaxKare treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                MaxKare describes itself as an ecommerce retailer of home comfort, massage, heated and baby-care products, and its treadmills are sold under its name. The brand does not name a manufacturer or parent company, and we could not confirm one. Like most marketplace brands, the machines are made by contract manufacturers and sold mainly through Walmart.com.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I buy a MaxKare treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mainly on Walmart.com, where most MaxKare treadmills are listed, and on the brand’s own store at maxkare.net. We found no MaxKare treadmill in Amazon’s US product data on 14 October 2026. On Walmart, check whether Walmart or a marketplace seller is selling the unit, because that decides who handles returns.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the MaxKare 3.5 HP treadmill good for running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for most people. It lists a 12 mph top speed, but its running surface is listed at 20 by 48 inches, and 48 inches is a walking length. Most adults need 55 inches or more to jog comfortably and about 60 to run. The 3.5 HP figure is not a continuous rating, so it cannot be compared with CHP figures on other brands.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit on a MaxKare treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the model, and some listings give two figures. The 20-inch model is titled 300 lb but its own text says 286 lb; a walking pad says 286 lb in its title and 265 lb in its text. Older models were listed at 220 lb. Plan around the lower figure on any listing, and leave a margin below it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What warranty does MaxKare offer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The store advertises an 18-month warranty in a banner, but its warranty page gives no length; it promises replacement of products defective due to manufacturer flaws and refunds within 30 days. The listings we read did not state a term either. Ask the seller for the length and coverage in writing before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Has MaxKare had any recalls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for treadmills. The CPSC recall database lists no MaxKare treadmill or walking pad recall as of 14 October 2026. In June 2025 about 8,560 MaxKare electric blankets sold exclusively on Walmart.com were recalled for overheating and fire hazards, with a refund remedy. That recall concerns a different product made by a different contract manufacturer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a MaxKare Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most disappointment with marketplace treadmills comes from trusting the listing more than it deserves. These are the four mistakes that matter most with MaxKare.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking the higher weight limit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Two MaxKare listings give two different limits for the same machine. Buyers who see 300 lb in the title miss the 286 lb in the text. Use the lower figure, keep your own weight well below it, and remember that a peak-rated motor has less headroom for a heavy user than its badge suggests.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for the top speed rather than the belt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 12 mph top speed looks like a running treadmill. A 48-inch belt is not one. Decide whether you will walk, jog or run, then check the running-surface length against that use before the speed figure. If you want to run, the belt length rules out the 20-inch model for most adults.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the warranty banner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The 18-month figure appears in a banner, not in the warranty terms, which give no length. If the motor or controller fails at month ten, the written terms are what you will be held to. Get the coverage from your seller in writing, including whether labour and shipping are included.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a sale item from the brand store without reading the refund policy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">MaxKare’s refund policy says sale items cannot be refunded and that buyers pay return shipping. On a treadmill that can make a change of mind expensive or impossible. Read the policy on the day you buy, and compare it with the return terms on the Walmart listing for the same machine.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

